Description: sensors that are found and opened while D-Bus calls come in
 iio-sensor-proxy takes its bus name, then finds and opens its sensors. The
 SSC drivers (a Qualcomm sensor core, through libssc) wait for the sensor
 core by running the main loop, so D-Bus calls come in while the sensors are
 found and opened:
 .
  - a claim of a sensor that was found but not open yet started polling on a
    device that was not there;
  - a claim made before the sensor was found never started it once it was;
  - the calls waiting for a sensor's start went into arrays not made yet;
  - a client that read HasAccelerometer (mutter) or HasCompass before the
    sensor was there never learned of it: the change went only to the clients
    that had claimed a sensor, and such a client claims only once it is true.
 .
 The arrays are now made with the clients' tables, before any call can come
 in. A claim of a sensor being opened waits for it, a sensor claimed while it
 was found or opened starts once it is open, and a sensor's Has property goes
 to every client on the bus whenever it changes (found, opened, gone). The
 readings still go only to the clients that claimed. A sensor removed, or the
 daemon stopped, while a sensor was still being opened no longer closes a
 device that was never opened.
 .
 tests/integration-test.py: a light sensor that comes and goes is announced
 to a client that claimed nothing.
